Once you have the page content and the specific element you need, `.value` will return it. For example, `a.value` retrieves the text inside the `<a>` tag, while `e.value` pulls the text from the `<img>` tag. This approach works on any page that loads the script, including sites using AJAX or other dynamic content injection.

The core idea behind this is straightforward: the code doesn't need to interact with the browser or perform any complex actions. It simply reads the raw data that the server has already sent to the page. This makes it highly effective for things like form submissions or data collection, where the goal is to grab whatever text the user types into a field or what appears on the screen.

The technique also works in reverse. If you already have the data you're looking for, you can just `.value` that specific element on the page. For instance, if you know the exact HTML tag that displays your target information, you can extract that directly without digging through the surrounding markup. This makes it a very efficient way to pull specific forms or lists of data out of a page, which is why it's heavily used in security testing and debugging.

